# A named law's `for` lines are its binders, `law doubled:` then `for n: nat` is `law doubled(n: nat): …`, as the law
# rules of every build read them (laws.wap, law_runs.wap, law_statements.wap)
rule law ($name: {for $binder {$type}; $lines...}) => law_for $name [$binder: $type] {$lines...}
rule law_for $name [$binders...] {for $binder {$type}; $lines...} => law_for $name [$binders..., $binder: $type] {$lines...}
rule law_for $name [$binders...] {$statement} => law($name: (($binders...) -> $statement))
# A law or a theorem with a proof, in every build, is the function giving the proof (wiki/proof.md), which a program
# may call: its result the statement, read as its truth, its body the proof the kernel checks (core/analyze/terms.wap),
# claimed to do nothing but compute its value, so a proof that may loop, raise or act is refused by the claim
# (core/analyze/effects.wap claims_checked), at its letters too (@proof, core/analyze/types.wap typed_alone)
# TODO (card rule-word-set) one rule each `where $word in [theorem, law]` once a rule's where takes more than one word
rule law($name($parameters...): $statement := $proof) => @proof $name($parameters...): $statement := $proof ! Pure
rule law($name: $statement := $proof) => @proof $name(): $statement := $proof ! Pure
rule theorem($name($parameters...): $statement := $proof) => @proof $name($parameters...): $statement := $proof ! Pure
rule theorem($name: $statement := $proof) => @proof $name(): $statement := $proof ! Pure
